# Keep the ETL service's build context small — this image only needs to run
# Python extractors, not the frontend or local data (data/ lives on a Railway
# volume, never baked into the image; see Dockerfile).
.git/
data/
# ...except the guard registry, which is not corpus. It is the DECLARATION of
# what a guard is, what code its verdict is bound to, and what exit codes it
# tolerates, and `ETL/lib/guard_verdicts.py` reads it at import to publish
# anything at all. Excluded with the rest of data/, it is absent from the image
# and absent from the volume, so every corpus-side guard the weekly cron runs
# dies before it can write a verdict -- and the census's own verdict block dies
# AFTER the census row is inserted, which is why corpus_census has rows from
# `etl-pipeline` and guard_verdicts has none from any producer but a laptop.
# Measured 2026-08-17: 783 verdicts from `local`, 5 from `proof-local`, 4 from
# a merge, and zero, ever, from the container.
#
# This takes effect on the next `railway up`; nothing redeploys the service on
# a push (repoTriggers: []). See project-mgmt/findings/gate_divergence_wsHC.md.
!data/guard_registry.json

# ...and the offerable baseline, for the same reason found the same way. Of the
# four guards `railway_cron.py::GUARDS` runs, `check_offerable_decay.py` reads
# `data/reference/offerable_baseline.json` -- committed, 1.5 KB, a REFERENCE
# input and not corpus. Excluded with the rest of `data/`, it is absent from the
# image and absent from the volume, so that guard would fail on the first run
# after the deploy that finally lets verdicts through.
#
# It fails differently from the registry and that difference is why it was
# missed: the registry is read at IMPORT, so it killed the verdict block
# silently; this is read inside a function, so it dies loudly when the guard
# runs. A loud failure on the first working run would have read as "the new
# channel is broken" rather than "one file is missing".
#
# The other three are clean: store_schema and corpus_marks touch no data/ path,
# and aggregate_independence reads data/db.sqlite, which IS the mounted volume.
#
# UNVERIFIED BY A BUILD, and say so rather than assume it. `.dockerignore`
# resolves by last-matching-pattern over the whole path -- it has no
# "don't descend into an excluded directory" rule, which is a .gitignore
# behaviour -- so a two-level re-inclusion under `data/` should work exactly as
# the one-level registry line above does. There is no docker CLI on this
# machine to prove it, and `COPY . .` at Dockerfile:26 means this file is the
# only thing governing it. **Check the image after the first `railway up`:** if
# check_offerable_decay still cannot find its baseline, the fix is to move the
# file out of `data/` rather than to fight the pattern.
!data/reference/offerable_baseline.json
website/frontend/node_modules/
website/frontend/.next/
website/frontend/out/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.DS_Store

# Secrets must never be baked into an image. `COPY . .` would otherwise carry
# .env.local into every layer of a registry image, and the file holds the
# Anthropic, OpenAI, Voyage and Postgres credentials. Railway injects the real
# environment at runtime; nothing in the image should need this file.
.env
.env.*
# Worktrees carry a `.git` FILE rather than a directory, which the `.git/`
# pattern above does not match, and a dangling symlink inside one fails the
# build with an unhelpful COPY error.
.git
.claude/

# A database backup must no more enter an image than a credential does. This
# directory is 123 MB of verified Postgres dumps (ETL/lib/backup_postgres.py),
# untracked and, until now, matched by nothing here -- so `COPY . .` would have
# baked it into every layer, more than doubling a 77 MB build context and
# putting a full copy of the corpus in a registry. Railway mounts the real data
# on a volume; nothing in this image should ever read a backup.
postgres-backup/

# corpus-ledger/ is the committed text copy of the authored instrument corpus.
# The ETL image never reads it (restore is a checkout operation), and it grows
# with every export -- 11 MB today. Keep it out of the build context.
corpus-ledger/
